<h1>Python Hashlib Guide: MD5, SHA-256, and File Hashes</h1>
<p>Python includes a standard library module called hashlib for calculating hashes. It is useful for file checksums, data fingerprints, cache keys, and compatibility tasks. It can calculate MD5, SHA-256, SHA-512, SHA-3, and other algorithms depending on the Python build and platform. For many developers, hashlib is the simplest safe starting point for non-password hashing.</p>
<p>The official <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html">Python hashlib documentation</a> explains that the module provides a common interface to secure hashes and message digests, including SHA algorithms and legacy algorithms such as MD5. That wording is important. MD5 is available, but availability does not mean it is suitable for every job.</p>
<p>For a short text value, the pattern is simple: create a hash object, pass bytes, and read the hexadecimal digest. Text must be encoded first, usually with UTF-8. For example, hashlib.sha256('hello'.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest() returns the SHA-256 digest of the word hello. For MD5, the pattern is similar, but use hashlib.md5 only when you need MD5 compatibility.</p>
<p>For files, read in chunks instead of loading the entire file into memory. This matters for ISO images, backups, videos, datasets, and large archives. Open the file in binary mode, read a block such as 1 MB, update the hash object, and repeat until the file ends. The final hexdigest is the file checksum.</p>
<p>A common beginner error is hashing the file name instead of the file contents. If you pass the string report.pdf into hashlib, you hash those characters, not the bytes inside report.pdf. To verify a download, you must open the file and hash its contents. This mistake explains many confusing checksum mismatches.</p>
<p>Another error is using Python hashes for passwords directly. Do not store hashlib.md5(password) or hashlib.sha256(password) as a login password hash. Password storage needs bcrypt, Argon2id, scrypt, or PBKDF2 with proper parameters. Python has libraries for those jobs, but hashlib's fast general hashes are not the right tool by themselves.</p>
<p>For April and May development cycles, hashlib is useful for release automation. A build script can calculate SHA-256 checksums for downloadable files and write them to a release manifest. Users can then verify downloads using the command-line methods described in <a href="/verify-file-checksums-windows-mac-linux.html">How to Verify File Checksums</a>.</p>
<p>For repeatable scripts, keep the algorithm name as a variable or command-line option. That makes it easy to calculate SHA-256 by default while still supporting MD5 for old manifests. Also print the algorithm beside the output. A bare hash value without context becomes confusing when someone reads the result months later.</p>
<p>When sharing Python examples publicly, label MD5 examples as legacy or compatibility examples. Beginners often copy code without the surrounding explanation. A short warning in the paragraph around the example can prevent MD5 from being reused for password storage or new trust-sensitive workflows.</p>
<p>Use SHA-256 as your normal default for new file checksums. Use MD5 only when matching legacy systems, old APIs, or historic checksum lists. The advantage of hashlib is that the code structure stays almost the same while the algorithm changes. That makes it easy to modernize old scripts one function call at a time.</p>
